{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"A\": \"Research cache-conscious integer radix sorting. Compare stable LSD variants using four 8-bit passes versus 11/11/10-bit passes, with sign-bit-transformed keys to preserve signed integer ordering. Use one reusable scratch array, explicit buffer ownership, and avoid an unnecessary final copy. Provide a small-input comparison-sort path and an allocation-failure fallback. If threading is already supported by the fixed build, compare a bounded four-worker variant using private histograms and stable, disjoint scatter offsets without per-element atomics. Measure complete-program performance rather than assuming fewer passes or more threads are faster.\",\n  \"B\": \"Research a robust comparison-sort alternative and radix fallback. Replace randomized Lomuto partitioning with median-of-three pivots and Hoare or block partitioning; add an insertion-sort cutoff, smaller-side recursion with tail iteration, and a depth-limited heapsort fallback. Evaluate duplicate-aware partitioning separately because its overhead may hurt ordinary inputs. Avoid shared RNG calls during parallel sorting. Where the existing build supports threading, test coarse tasks within one bounded four-worker team, retaining a serial cutoff. Do not introduce compiler flags or runtime dependencies.\",\n  \"C\": \"Research exact case-generation acceleration and producer/sorter fusion. First explore inlining, unrolling, and independent-state interleaving without changing floating-point evaluation. For each multiply-with-carry state, investigate exact skip-ahead using s' congruent to a*s modulo a*65536-1, proving applicability to the supplied seeds and chunk boundaries. Independent contiguous chunks could support interleaving or existing four-worker facilities. Preserve unsigned wrapping, conversion behavior, the original drand multiplication/division/truncation, and final RNG state when retaining the original sort. Separately test accumulating A's radix histograms during generation. Do not assume drand can be replaced by an integer RNG result merely because its scale constants match.\",\n  \"G\": \"Integrate measured winners, not presumed additive improvements. Establish each tree's standalone correctness and end-to-end timing through the unchanged automatic harness, then test the strongest sort with C's generator and histogram-fusion variants. Resolve signed-key handling, histogram layout, scratch-buffer ownership, allocation fallback, and thread-pool compatibility; avoid nested pools and oversubscription. Preserve input/error behavior, sortedness semantics, conditional MD5 computation, raw-array hashing, and output formatting. Compare integrations against the best standalone candidate and retain that candidate if integration regresses. Use only general input-size thresholds; keep tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, and permissions unchanged.\",\n  \"analysis\": \"The active source implements Problem B. Its baseline passes four correctness cases and averages 0.06809694 seconds across three tightly clustered samples. No input sizes, phase timings, or optimized measurements are supplied, so speedup claims would be speculative. The branch-heavy Lomuto sort, serial deterministic generator, and repeated memory passes motivate independent sorting and generation research. ARM64 with four CPU cores favors portable, low-overhead implementations; thread startup and allocation may matter at this runtime. Every candidate must generate the reference integer sequence, actually sort it, and hash the resulting array bytes—not substitute expected digests. Generation's unsigned arithmetic, abs conversion, and floating-point mapping require particular care.\"\n}"}}
